C is out of question, as there would be no good reason for the new
code to be better than the old code.  I don't think sticking to the
language that's gonna attract more developers is wise.  Picking the
best language for the specific task is the way to go, and that I think
would be C++, Python or D, in that order (I don't like Java too much).

> Do you mean 2 to be something like "write a new rogue-like based on
> NetHack gameplay from scratch" or instead "start with NetHack sources,
> but bear in mind that the first projects are to rewrite it piece-wise"?

In my opinion the best bet would be to create a roguelike engine from
scratch (or start with some modern, well designed engine), and put the
nethack content on top.  Ideally, make a game that's identical to
3.4.3 from the player's perspective.

With this approach the effort could be split up into 3 separate areas:
- writing the engine
- organizing the current content for use with the engine
- organizing YANIs, designing what would later become a new version.


